// Fixture: nested_bare_scalar_field_error
// Rule under test: resolved by lead session (previously flagged as underspecified).
// Bare `field` (no `=`) syntax means "enter the field's existing scope, touch only what's
// listed" (§6.4). That meaning presupposes a scope to enter. Scalar-typed fields have no
// scope -- there is nothing to enter -- so writing a scalar field in bare form is a
// compile-time error.
//
// Phasing note (relayed from lead session, worth keeping in Compiler Core too): this is
// a PHASE 5 (Validate, per §12) error, not a parse-time one. The parser alone cannot
// distinguish "bare struct-typed field" from "bare scalar-typed field" -- both are
// syntactically identical (a field name, no `=`, followed by an indented block). The
// distinction only becomes checkable once field types are known, which happens at
// Register (phase 4) and is checked at Validate (phase 5). This fixture's error should
// therefore surface as a validation-stage error, not a parser/syntax-stage error --
// worth confirming Compiler Core's error actually lands in that phase and not earlier.

define Payload
	a = u32
	b = u32

define Container
	payload = Payload
	label   = u32

Container Container_Seed delete
	payload
		a = 1
		b = 2
	label = 10

Container Holder = Container_Seed
	label
		// INVALID: label is scalar (u32) -- there is no scope here to enter.
		// Expected: compile error at phase 5 (Validate), not phase 3 (Parse).
		anything = 5

// Expected (prediction, pending golden output from Compiler Core reference run):
//   Compile error, reported against Holder's `label` statement.
//   Error should be attributable to phase 5 (type validation: bare-field form requires
//   a struct-typed field; `label` is scalar), NOT phase 3 (parsing succeeds -- the
//   bare-field-with-indented-block shape is syntactically well-formed on its own).
